22:43:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The simple answer is that organ rejection is a T‑cell‑driven response against foreign **MHC molecules** (HLA) expressed on donor cells, while donated red blood cells lack these molecules entirely and the main blood‑group antigens are carbohydrate structures that are easily matched. Here’s a more detailed breakdown: --- ### 1. Organ transplants – a cellular graft with “self” markers Solid organs (kidney, heart, liver, etc.) are complex, living tissues that contain: - **Parenchymal cells** (the functional cells of the organ) - **Passenger leukocytes** (dendritic cells, macrophages, lymphocytes) carried within the graft All nucleated cells in the body display **MHC class I** (HLA‑A, ‑B, ‑C) on their surface, and professional antigen‑presenting cells also express **MHC class II** (HLA‑DP, ‑DQ, ‑DR). These molecules are highly polymorphic—each person’s set is slightly different. The recipient’s immune system constantly patrols for “non‑self” MHC. When it encounters a donor organ, two things can happen: - **Direct allorecognition:** recipient T cells directly recognise intact donor MHC molecules as foreign, even without processing. - **Indirect allorecognition:** recipient antigen‑presenting cells take up donor proteins, process them, and present donor‑derived peptides on recipient MHC. Both pathways activate T cells, which orchestrate an attack—cytotoxic T cells kill donor cells, helper T cells drive inflammation and antibody production, and the graft is gradually destroyed. This is what we call **rejection**. Because thousands of HLA variants exist, a perfect match is extremely rare, so lifelong immunosuppression is required. --- ### 2. Blood transfusions – mainly a fluid of non‑nucleated cells A standard red‑blood‑cell transfusion consists almost entirely of **red blood cells** (RBCs) suspended in plasma. The critical differences are: - **RBCs are anucleate and do not express MHC I or II.** Without MHC, they cannot directly show a foreign “self” signal to T cells. Thus the powerful T‑cell‑mediated rejection that targets organ grafts doesn’t occur against RBCs. - **The major antigens on RBCs are carbohydrates**, not proteins. The ABO and Rh systems are the most important. These carbohydrate antigens can be recognised by **pre‑existing antibodies** (naturally occurring anti‑A, anti‑B, or formed after previous exposure). If you transfuse ABO‑incompatible blood, the recipient’s antibodies immediately bind the donor RBCs, causing intravascular haemolysis—a **transfusion reaction**. This is a purely antibody‑mediated event, not a T‑cell‑mediated rejection. Because the ABO/Rh systems are simple and we always cross‑match, this risk is effectively eliminated. - **Leukoreduction (white‑cell removal)** is now standard practice. Even the few white blood cells in a unit of blood could, in theory, stimulate an immune response (HLA alloimmunisation). By filtering them out, the chance of T‑cell activation is reduced virtually to zero. - **Blood is not a vascularised graft.** It doesn’t need to integrate into the recipient’s circulation—it simply mixes and flows. There is no “organ” structure to be invaded by recipient immune cells. --- ### 3. Why we don’t call it “rejection” for blood In medical terminology, “rejection” is reserved for the complex cellular and humoral process that destroys a solid transplanted organ. The acute haemolytic catastrophe caused by an ABO mismatch is instead called an **acute haemolytic transfusion reaction**, and it is avoided by careful blood typing and cross‑matching. Once ABO and Rh are compatible, the risk of any immune‑mediated destruction of transfused RBCs becomes negligible because the T‑cell‑dependent arm of the immune system has nothing to latch onto. --- **In short:** Organs teem with cells carrying foreign MHC, which directly triggers T‑cell rejection. Red blood cells carry no MHC, only a handful of easy‑to‑match carbohydrate antigens, so a simple antibody‑mediated reaction is the only threat—and standard matching prevents it.
